<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>Enactive.Application</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    What a host does around a run that is neither the engine nor an interface: starting and stopping
    runs, the background group, decisions and the inbox, schedules, saving settings (ADR 0002 §2).

    It lives outside Enactive.App.Ui for the same reason Enactive.Settings does: App.Ui is a WinExe and
    nothing else can reference it. While this code lived there, the only way a test could reach it was
    to compile the window's source files into the test assembly one by one, and a second host (the
    service, ADR 0002) could not reach it at all - it would have had to grow its own copy, and two copies
    of the rules around a run drift exactly where nobody looks.

    NO AVALONIA HERE, and nothing else that draws. Enactive.Engine.Tests checks the references of this
    assembly (ApplicationLayerTests), so a convenience import of a UI package fails a test rather than
    quietly making the service depend on a desktop toolkit.

    Projections/ holds what is computed from a run for showing it - the timeline, which model served each
    phase, the models' performance, the local/cloud split, what an unfinished run left behind. They
    describe the run, so every host shows the same figures; how one person looks at it (a card folded
    open) is not here and stays with the host that draws it. They raise change notifications on the
    thread that changed them; marshalling to a UI thread is the host's (see Mvvm/ObservableObject).

    The namespace is Enactive.Application, which hides Avalonia's Application type from any file that
    imports Avalonia OUTSIDE its namespace declaration: walking out of Enactive.App.Ui, the compiler
    finds the namespace Enactive.Application before it gets to the file's usings. The three places in
    App.Ui that name that type write it as Avalonia.Application for that reason.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Enactive.Core\Enactive.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Enactive.Agents\Enactive.Agents.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Enactive.Settings\Enactive.Settings.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Enactive.Workspace\Enactive.Workspace.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The types here are internal, as they were in the window; these are the assemblies that use them.
         enactive-ui is App.Ui's AssemblyName, which is what InternalsVisibleTo matches, not the project name. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="enactive-ui" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Enactive.App.Ui.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Enactive.Engine.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
